&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What AI Search Visibility Means&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;AI search visibility is a brand’s presence inside AI-generated answers — the citations, summaries, and recommendations that AI engines like ChatGPT, Perplexity, &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-overviews"&gt;Google AI Overviews&lt;/a&gt;, and Gemini produce in response to user queries.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;AI Search Visibility vs. Traditional Rank Tracking&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Traditional rank tracking measures where a webpage appears in a list of blue links. AI search visibility measures whether a brand’s content is retrieved, synthesized, and surfaced as a trusted source inside a generated response.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The core difference is structure. In traditional search, ranking depends heavily on backlinks, domain authority, and keyword alignment. In AI search, visibility depends on whether an &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-search-engines"&gt;AI search engine&lt;/a&gt; can confidently interpret, extract, and attribute a brand’s content.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A page can hold a strong position in Google’s organic results and still be absent from AI-generated answers simply because AI systems prioritize clarity, entity authority, and cross-platform consistency over ranking signals alone.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Featured Resource: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ran-ai-search-experiments"&gt;I Ran 100s of Queries to Find Out if AI Chatbots Are Better Than Search Engines&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Citations, Share of Voice, and Sentiment&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Three metrics define AI search visibility in practice.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Citations&lt;/strong&gt; measure how often an AI engine names or links a brand as a source in a generated answer.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Share of voice&lt;/strong&gt; tracks how frequently a brand appears relative to competitors across a specific topic or category.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sentiment&lt;/strong&gt; captures how AI engines characterize a brand, whether the description is positive, neutral, or negative, and whether it aligns with intended positioning.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Together, these three metrics provide more complete insight into AI search visibility than any single metric alone. A brand can have high citation frequency but poor sentiment, or low share of voice but strong positioning in the citations it does earn. Understanding all three is what makes AI visibility measurement actionable.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Where Semrush Fits In AI Visibility&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Semrush added AI visibility tracking as an extension of its existing SEO suite, layering AI Overview monitoring, limited citation insights, and keyword data for AI‑influenced queries in addition to its traditional tooling. For teams already working inside Semrush for technical SEO, backlinks, and competitive research, this reduces tool switching and keeps everything in a single workspace.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Its AI visibility features lean heavily toward Google AI Overviews, Gemini, ChatGPT, Perplexity, and traditional search integrations. Semrush’s AI visibility features do not cover emerging assistants like Meta AI, Copilot, or Yahoo Scout. This is a gap for teams that need broader cross‑engine visibility.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;And for organizations not already standardized on Semrush, pricing remains one of the biggest barriers to adopting its AI layer.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Semrush AI Visibility Alternatives to Evaluate&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;1. &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/aeo"&gt;HubSpot AEO&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/ai%20search%20strategy%20built%20with%20hubspot%20aeo.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=366&amp;amp;name=ai%20search%20strategy%20built%20with%20hubspot%20aeo.webp" width="650" height="366" alt="semrush ai visibility alternatives, HubSpot AEO" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HubSpot AEO (Answer Engine Optimization) is built specifically to track and improve brand visibility inside AI-generated answers. Where most SEO tools treat AI visibility as an add-on, HubSpot AEO is designed around it. The tool connects citation data, share of voice, and competitor comparisons directly to the CRM context and content tools teams already use.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What it tracks:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Brand mentions and citations across ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Share of voice versus named competitors across specific topics&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;How AI engines describe and position the brand&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Sentiment and accuracy of AI-generated brand representation&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What makes it different:&lt;/strong&gt; HubSpot AEO connects visibility data to &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/marketing"&gt;Marketing Hub&lt;/a&gt;‘s campaign reporting and &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/products/crm/ai-crm"&gt;Smart CRM’&lt;/a&gt;s pipeline and revenue attribution. That means a demand gen team can see not just where the brand appears in AI answers, but whether those AI-influenced touchpoints are converting into contacts, deals, and pipeline.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/aeo-grader"&gt;AEO Grader&lt;/a&gt; — HubSpot’s free baseline tool — provides teams with a benchmark before committing to a full platform. It scores AI visibility across major engines and surfaces where competitors are being cited instead. Smart CRM integration is the piece that closes the attribution loop for RevOps teams who need to tie A...

&lt;p&gt;AI content is polarizing. When users spot that AI was used in art, backlash often follows. So, let’s run a quick experiment.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=7348143f-fd94-4a49-9caa-6a82f9541f3d&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Download&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;free introductory guide to marketing psychology here.&amp;nbsp;" height="60" width="633" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/7348143f-fd94-4a49-9caa-6a82f9541f3d.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Take a look at the picture below. I just generated an image, in the style of a Monet painting, using AI. I used the latest AI models trained on the world’s best artwork. Have a look, and think about what makes this image inferior to a real Monet painting. Think about what you like and what you don’t like about it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hubfs/53/monet-2-20260730-8456081.webp" width="0" height="0" alt="Monet painting" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p style="text-align: center; font-size: 12px;"&gt;&lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_Lilies_(Monet_series)"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Source&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Now, what if I told you that I lied? The image is a real Monet. A few weeks back, X user &lt;a href="https://x.com/SHL0MS/status/2054280631807316329"&gt;SHLOMS&lt;/a&gt; performed the same exercise. Let’s talk about what happened.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#the-post-that-started-it-all"&gt;The Post That Started It All&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#paint"&gt;The “AI” Painting That Fooled Thousands&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The Post That Started It All&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/i%20just%20generated%20an%20image%20in%20the%20style%20of%20a%20monet%20painting%20using%20ai%20please%20describe%2c%20in%20as%20much%20detail%20as%20possible%2c%20what%20makes%20this%20inferior%20to%20a%20real%20monet%20painting.webp?width=450&amp;amp;height=588&amp;amp;name=i%20just%20generated%20an%20image%20in%20the%20style%20of%20a%20monet%20painting%20using%20ai%20please%20describe%2c%20in%20as%20much%20detail%20as%20possible%2c%20what%20makes%20this%20inferior%20to%20a%20real%20monet%20painting.webp" width="450" height="588" alt="i just generated an image in the style of a monet painting using ai please describe, in as much detail as possible, what makes this inferior to a real monet painting" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 450px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p style="text-align: center; font-size: 12px;"&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/SHL0MS/status/2054280631807316329"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Source&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Over 1,300 people responded to SHLOMS’s post with resoundingly similar feedback. Almost everyone who replied said this AI art was pure crap.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;They said it lacked “coherent composition.” The colors were an “incoherent muddle.” Some said it was “trying too hard&lt;em&gt;.”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Others called the image “obvious AI slop.” They called it “garbage” and “borked nonsense with no sense of space.” Users picked holes in every aspect of the painting. They questioned its lack of meaning and how AI art simply lacks the magical quality imbued by human creativity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But there’s a problem … this Monet painting is real.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;The “AI” Painting That Fooled Thousands&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;SHOLMS lied. It was their version of a social experiment. They took a real (much-loved) Monet painting, pretended it was an AI-generated image, and then gauged the reaction.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;I’ll be honest. My gut reaction to the X post was the same. I immediately thought it was slop. I tried to spot flaws and thought the colors were dull. And it turns out that all of us do this.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;When we think art is created by AI, we tend to dislike it. In fact, when we think anything took no effort to build, we dislike it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We follow what &lt;a href="https://search.asu.edu/profile/837523"&gt;Andrea Morales&lt;/a&gt;, Lonnie L. Ostrom Chair in Business and professor in the Department of Marketing at Arizona State University, calls an “effort heuristic.” In other words, if something took two seconds to generate, we’ll hate it. If it took 15 years of craft to perfect, we’ll love it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In a 2005 study published in the &lt;a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/23547222_Giving_Firms_an_E_for_Effort_Consumer_Responses_to_High-Effort_Firms"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Journal of Consumer Research&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, Morales found that people will generally reward actions that took a great deal of effort. The study followed 46 participants who were in the market for a new home. They were told to hire a real estate agent to help them search. The agent reviewed their preferences and put together a personalized list of 10 apartments.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;However, 23 of the participants were told the agent “did everything manually and spent nine hours building the list.” The other 23 were told that ​“the agent used a computer program, spending only one hour on the list.”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/nudge%2c%20taking%20time%20to%20create%20a%20listing.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=406&amp;amp;name=nudge%2c%20taking%20time%20to%20create%20a%20listing.webp" width="650" height="406" alt="nudge, taking time to create a listing" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In reality, the houses on both lists were identical. The real estate agent lied about how much time they spent on the search. It always took one hour.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But perception was swayed by the perceived effort. Participants who were told that the list took nine hours to create rated the houses 30.5% higher. We use effort as a proxy to measure quality — especially when quality is hard to measure.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/nudge%2c%20real%20estate%20list%20quality%20score.webp?width=650&amp;amp;height=406&amp;amp;name=nudge%2c%20real%20estate%20list%20quality%20score.webp" width="650" height="406" alt="nudge, real estate list quality score" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So when 6.8 million people saw an image of a real Monet painting but thought it was AI-generated, they immediately called it slop.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This presents a tricky contradiction. We’re repeatedly told that to compete, we need to embrace AI. But the Morales study shows a different perspective.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;If we only use AI (or even if people &lt;em&gt;think&lt;/em&gt; we only use AI), people will feel an urge to hate our work. The fantastic copywriter Dave Harland calls this “&lt;a href="https://dashboard.mailerlite.com/forms/89046/59435506742592839/share"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Death By Sepia&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://53.fs1.hubspotusercontent-na1.net/hub/53/hubfs/monet-6-20260730-2662866.webp?width=450&amp;amp;height=479&amp;amp;name=monet-6-20260730-2662866.webp" width="450" height="479" alt="bluebelll restaurant bil...

&lt;p&gt;Here’s the question most teams are actually trying to answer: do you need a standalone tool to monitor how your brand shows up in AI search, or do you need something that connects that monitoring to the content and CRM workflows that let you act on it?&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;That’s the real difference between HubSpot AEO and Otterly. Both track your visibility in AI engines like ChatGPT, Gemini, and Perplexity. But what happens after the data comes in is where they diverge.&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;HubSpot AEO is a CRM-connected AEO solution built directly into the HubSpot platform. It surfaces visibility insights and routes them into content creation, campaign management, and pipeline tracking, without requiring you to switch tools. Otterly is a standalone AEO monitoring tool that tracks your AI visibility, provides reporting, and lets you export data via Looker Studio, an API, or an MCP server.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;If you’re trying to decide between them, here’s an honest breakdown.&lt;/p&gt; 
